<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kashgar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kashi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kashgar is an oasis city in the far west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, near the borders of Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> routes across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> met those around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. For centuries a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> Muslim culture and trade, it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>, the Chagatai khans and the Khoja leaders, paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> from 1680 and was conquered by the Qing in 1759.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashgar.jpg" alt="Kashgar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N509FZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%ABytgah_Mosque,_Kashi_(20230923100109).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1680 the cities of Altishahr, among them <b>Kashgar</b>, Yarkand and Khotan, paid the Dzungars substantial tribute in grain, cloth and silver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The early rulers, who held Balasagun in the Chu valley and <b>Kashgar</b> in the western Tarim Basin, are known mainly from later Muslim traditions and from coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Repelled at <b>Kashgar</b> in 1131, he extended his authority over Qayaliq and Almaliq, and in 1134 took the Karakhanid city of Balasaghun, which became his capital; the Kankalis, Karluks, Kyrgyz and the Kingdom of Qocho became vassals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Shah I (r. 1072–1092) the empire campaigned across Syria, Anatolia, the Caucasus and Central Asia, receiving the submission of the Karakhanids of Samarkand and <b>Kashgar</b> and reaching an estimated maximum extent of some 3.9 million square kilometres, from the Mediterranean coast to the borders of the Chinese world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west, the Karakhanids, who ruled <b>Kashgar</b> and the lands around it from the 10th century, became the first Turkic dynasty to adopt Islam, and their language, close to Old Turkic, became a language of Islamic literature written in Arabic script.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Kashgar became a centre of Turkic Islamic culture: Yusuf Balasaguni presented his mirror for princes, the Kutadgu Bilig, to the <b>Kashgar</b> khan around 1069.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-kara-khanid-khanate">Eastern Kara-Khanid Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
